      "title": "Position Title Student Accessibility Specialist",
      "description": " \n UNIVERSITY OF SCIENCE AND ARTS OF OKLAHOMA \nOffice of Human Resources \n \nNOTICE OF VACANCY \nPosition Title Student Accessibility Specialist \nSalary $42,000 – $46,000; commensurate with experience \nReports To Dean of Students \nBenefits USAO offers a comprehensive benefits package including retirement, \nhealth, dental, vision and life insurance. \nPosition Type Full-time, Staff | FLSA Exempt \nHours Monday – Friday, 8:00 a.m. – 5:00 p.m.; occasional evenings and \nweekends \nSupervisory Duties None \nOpen Until Until Filled \n \n  Position Summary \nThe University of Science and Arts of Oklahoma (USAO) seeks a full-time Student Accessibility \nSpecialist to ensure equal access to academic programs, university housing, and campus \nfacilities for students with disabilities. Operating under Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, the \nAmericans with Disabilities Act (ADA), and the Fair Housing Act (FHA), this position serves as the \nprimary administrator for all student accommodation requests, with specialized oversight of \nEmotional Support Animal (ESA) and service animal requests, housing access, and campus \nassistance animal compliance. The Specialist evaluates documentation, conducts interactive \nintakes, manages the full lifecycle of ESA approvals, and collaborates closely with Housing & \nResidence Life, Facilities, and Campus Health to support student success. \n  Essential Duties & Responsibilities \nThe following duties are representative of the essential functions of this position. This list is not \nexhaustive, and other duties may be assigned as needed. \n• ESA Request Administration: Manage the end-to-end evaluation and review process \nfor Emotional Support Animal (ESA) requests in campus housing under the Fair Housing \nAct (FHA) and institutional guidelines. \n• Documentation & Verification: Review and verify clinical documentation from \nqualified healthcare providers to establish the connection between a student’s \ndisability and the therapeutic necessity of an ESA. \n• ESA Orientation & Agreements: Issue ESA approval documentation; conduct \nmandatory pre-approval orientation sessions on housing rules and handler \nresponsibilities; and ensure signed owner agreements, local municipal licenses, and \ncurrent veterinary and vaccination records are maintained on file. \n• Interdepartmental Housing Coordination: Partner with Housing & Residence Life, \nFacilities Management, and Campus Safety to log approved ESAs, designate animal-\nfriendly housing units, resolve roommate and allergy conflicts, and address ESA policy \nviolations or behavioral concerns. \n• Service Animal Support: Serve as the campus point of contact and subject matter \nexpert for service animals, guiding faculty and staff on proper ADA inquiry protocols. \n• Intake & Interactive Evaluation: Conduct intake appointments with prospective, \nincoming, and enrolled students to identify functional limitations, learning barriers, and \nacademic needs. \n• Documentation Review: Review and interpret psychoeducational, psychological, and \nmedical evaluations to determine eligibility for academic accommodations. \n• Accommodation Planning: Authorize and implement individualized academic \naccommodations, such as testing modifications, alternative-format media, note-taking \nassistance, and adaptive technology. \n• Faculty Collaboration: Advise and assist faculty in implementing classroom \naccommodations while ensuring fundamental course objectives and academic \nstandards are maintained. \n• Progress Monitoring: Maintain ongoing contact with registered students and evaluate \nthe effectiveness of academic and residential accommodation plans each term. \n• Student Self-Advocacy: Coach students on self-advocacy skills, navigating university \nprocesses, and transitioning to college-level learning expectations. \n• Conflict Resolution: Facilitate resolution strategies when accommodation disputes, \nclassroom barriers, or residential animal concerns arise. \n• Resource Coordination: Oversee alternative-text conversion, sign language \ninterpreting, captioning services, and specialized testing environments. \n• Confidential Record Keeping: Maintain accurate, auditable, and confidential \nelectronic student records in compliance with FERPA, HIPAA, and federal record \nretention requirements. \n• Case Management Systems: Track and process all academic, housing, and ESA \nrequests using accessibility case management software or the student information \nsystem to log cases, issue accommodation letters, and track annual animal renewals. \n• Training & Outreach: Design and present training for Residence Life staff (Resident \nAssistants and Hall Directors), faculty, and campus partners on ESA policies, ADA \ncompliance, and Universal Design for Learning (UDL). \n• Orientation & Events: Represent Student Accessibility Services at recruiting events, \nnew-student orientation, and campus resource fairs. \n• Policy Support: Assist departmental leadership in updating operational manuals, ESA \nhousing policies, and accommodation request workflows. \n• Perform other related duties as assigned. \n  Skills & Abilities \n \n• Knowledge of Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, Titles II and III of the ADA, the Fair \nHousing Act (FHA) as applied to university housing, and FERPA. \n• Knowledge of disability documentation standards and the interactive process for \nacademic and housing accommodations. \n• Familiarity with Universal Design for Learning (UDL) principles and assistive and \nadaptive technology. \n• Ability to evaluate medical and psychological documentation objectively, \ndistinguishing valid clinical needs from non-qualifying requests. \n• Strong interpersonal, boundary-setting, and de-escalation skills to handle high-\nemotion requests, communicate difficult decisions, and manage student and parent \nexpectations constructively. \n• Ability to build effective working relationships with Housing & Residence Life, Facilities, \nLegal Counsel, and Student Affairs leadership. \n• Strong organizational skills to track animal health records, vaccination renewals, and \nhousing compliance timelines. \n• Ability to maintain strict confidentiality and exercise sound judgment with sensitive \ninformation. \n• Proficiency with case management platforms, student information systems (e.g., \nEllucian Colleague), and learning management systems (e.g., Canvas). \n  Required Qualifications \n \n• Bachelor’s degree in Special Education, Rehabilitation Counseling, Higher Education \nAdministration, Social Work, Counseling, Psychology, or a related field. \n• Minimum of two (2) years of experience administering disability accommodations or \ncase management. \n• Thorough understanding of Section 504, Titles II and III of the ADA, the Fair Housing Act \n(FHA) as applied to higher education housing, and FERPA. \n• Strong written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to present training \nto faculty, staff, and students. \n  Preferred Qualifications \n \n• Master’s degree in Higher Education, Student Personnel, Clinical Counseling, \nVocational Rehabilitation, or a related field. \n• Accommodation or case management experience within higher education or \nresidential life environments. \n• Prior experience evaluating Fair Housing Act (FHA) assistance animal requests or \nmanaging university residential accommodation processes. \n• Experience with disability case management platforms (e.g., Accommodate, \nClockWork) and higher education systems such as Ellucian Colleague and Canvas. \n  Physical Demands \nThe physical demands described here are representative of those that must be met by an \nemployee to successfully perform the essential functions of this job. Reasonable \naccommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential \nfunctions. \n• Prolonged periods of sitting at a desk and working on a computer. \n• Ability to move between campus buildings, including residence halls, for meetings, \nconsultations, and events. \n• Ability to occasionally lift and carry up to 20 pounds, such as presentation or outreach \nmaterials. \n• Frequent verbal and written communication with students, families, faculty, and staff. \n  Work Environment \nThe work environment characteristics described here are representative of those an employee \nencounters while performing the essential functions of this job. Reasonable accommodations \nmay be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. \nThis position operates in a standard office environment with a moderate noise level. Work \nincludes visits to residence halls and other campus facilities, and the employee may occasionally \nbe in proximity to service animals and emotional support animals. Occasional evening or \nweekend work may be required for orientation, recruiting events, and training sessions. \n  About USAO \nThe University of Science and Arts of Oklahoma (USAO) is Oklahoma’s only public liberal arts \nuniversity, located in Chickasha, Oklahoma, approximately 40 miles southwest of Oklahoma City. \nA proud member of the Council of Public Liberal Arts Colleges (COPLAC), USAO is charged by the \nOklahoma State Regents for Higher Education to provide an outstanding general education \nprogram with strong offerings in the liberal arts and sciences. For more information, visit \nwww.usao.edu. \n  To Apply \nComplete the USAO application at https://usao.edu/about/human-resources/careers-at-\nusao.html \n \nWhen completing the USAO application, please attach: \n• A letter of interest \n• A detailed resume \n• Three (3) professional/academic references \n• Any necessary transcripts \n \nIf you have any questions, please email careers@usao.edu or visit the Office of Human Resources \nin person: \nUniversity of Science & Arts of Oklahoma | Troutt Hall, Room 306 | 1727 W. Alabama Ave., \nChickasha, OK 73018 \n \n \nAN AFFIRMATIVE ACTION / EQUAL OPPORTUNITY EMPLOYER \nThis institution, in compliance with Titles VI and VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, Executive Order \n11246 as amended, Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Section 504 of the \nRehabilitation Act of 1973, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, and other federal laws and \nregulations, does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, age, religion, \ndisability, or status as a veteran in any of its policies, practices, or procedures. This includes, but \nis not limited to, admissions, employment, financial aid, and educational services. \nAs required by the US Department of Education, employees are required to report violations under \nTitle IX and, under the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Crime Statistics Act \n(Clery Act), select individuals are required to report crimes. If this position is identified as a \nCampus Security Authority (Clery Act), you will be notified, trained, and provided with resources \nfor reporting. \n "
    }
